  the 35 gigawatt hour cell production for  this purpose let's analyze how many Giga  factories the grid would need to become  stable under 100 percent renewable  energy keeping a mind that we do not  need to produce all of this in one year  the European Renewable Energy Council  optimistically targeted 2050 as the year  that Europe could generate 100 percent  of its energy with renewable energy so  let's say we have 33 years to produce  the required storage  that statistic of a 1 gigawatt hour  battery supporting New York for 270  seconds should give you an idea of how  much battery storage will be needed to  support a city but not all the  electricity will come from battery much  of it will come directly from the power  source as it's being generated there  really is no reason why we could not  rely completely on renewable energy  supported by batteries and pumped  storage and the European Renewable  Energy Council has suggested this  breakdown of energy sources to reach  that goal by 2050 let's first look at  that suggested wind power capacity at  462 gigawatts we can estimate how much  storage this would need by taking a look  at Australia's new horn sail wind firm  which is supported by the world's  largest battery storage facility  consisting of 129 megawatt hours of  storage provided by the Tesla power pack  this storage is there to allow the 99  wind turbines with a capacity of 315  megawatts to provide stable power to the  grid removing the problems of variable  wind speeds scaling that 315 megawatts  to 462 gigawatts would require one  thousand four hundred and sixty seven  times more storage or a hundred and  eighty nine gigawatt hours of storage a  single Giga factory could produce that  in less than four years hydro biomass  concentrated solar power and geothermal  would require no battery storage as they  are relatively stable over the course of  a day ocean energy would require similar  storage to wind concentrated solar power  can store its energy in molten salt and  geothermal in particular has an  incredibly stable capacity factor even  beating coal the capacity factor tells  us an energy sources ability to deliver  on its maximum possible output Solar has  one of the lowest capacity factors over  the course of a single day because it  generates zero percent of its potential  power at night and thus requires the  most energy storage as a case study for  how much storage we would need here we  can take Tesla's project in Hawaii to  provide the isolated community of Kauai  with a solar powered micro grid the  Kauai project consists of a 30 megawatt  solar farm paired with a 52 megawatt  hour battery installation already we can  see that the ratio of batteries  our capacity is much higher and this is  on a tropical island in the Pacific  Ocean Europe would likely need a higher  capacity to make up for less Sun which  would likely reduce the ratio of  capacity to storage this may skew  results nevertheless scaling that 13  mega watt solar firm to 962 gigawatts  would require 74 thousand times more  storage where 3848 gigawatt hours of  storage adding in our storage needs for  wind and ocean we get a total of four  thousand and sixty four gigawatt hours  for Europe alone Europe currently makes  up about 13.3% of the world's energy  demand so an estimate follows  assumptions the battery storage the  world would need is thirty thousand five  hundred and fifty six gigawatt hours  that much battery storage would allow  New York to run at peak usage for ninety  five days straight without any  additional power which would take  eighteen point five gigafactory  33 years to manufacture and with current  prices per kilowatt hour of storage cost  three point eight trillion dollars this  lines up neatly with Tesla's goal to  build 10 to 20 Giga factories with 18  Giga factories running at current  production targets Tesla would be able  to produce enough carrots to overtake  Ford's current market share in motor  vehicles with 9 million vehicles and  allowed Tesla to produce enough battery  storage to help transition the world to  a hundred percent renewable grid over  the next 33 years another company  working to power the world is Ankur an  industry leader in mobile charging  founded by former Google employees who  are currently holding a competition for  you to win two thousand dollars and an  anchor power pack by simply creating a  one-minute video where you share an  awkward situation caused by your devices  running out of power the top ten videos  will win the prize for entry details  just follow the link in the description  Ankur's power banks are simply the best  I have ever used I've been using this  20,000 milliamp hour battery and it  charges my phone faster than any of the  other power banks I've used thanks to  anchors fast charging technologies this  power bank has enough battery to charge  my iPhone seven times over or it could  charge my phone  earphones and iPad pro once it's the  perfect travel companion and I always  carried in my bag and it only costs  forty dollars right now on Amazon it's  an absolute steal at that price I can't  recommend it enough I've link to it in  the description as well thanks to  minutephysics for collaborating with me  on this video be sure to check out his  video right now we also just released  the latest episode of the show makers  podcast with many physics other guests  so if you'd like to learn more about how  Henry created minutephysics be sure to  check that out too
 

